\title{Learning inter-family correlations with a shared hidden layer:\\
a two-family Ising model as a controlled test case}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We consider a toy statistical-mechanics system made of two weakly coupled
Ising subsystems (``families''), each with its own sparse random
intra-family couplings and a much sparser set of inter-family couplings.
We ask whether a Restricted Boltzmann Machine (RBM), trained only on
family-resolved data, can be extended with a small number of additional
hidden units to recover the inter-family coupling structure without
disturbing what has already been learned about each family in isolation.
The extension is built by freezing the parameters of two independently
trained RBMs inside a larger, paired RBM and leaving only a small block of
newly added hidden units, connected to the full joint visible layer, free
to adapt. We find that, for a sufficiently dense inter-family coupling
matrix, these added units do recover genuine cross-family structure: the
correlation matrix sampled from the trained paired model at equilibrium
correlates with the true (hidden) coupling matrix at $r\simeq0.80$,
essentially saturating the finite-sample ceiling set by the data
themselves ($r\simeq0.84$), and far above the $r\simeq0$ obtained from two
independent, uncoupled RBMs. The result is essentially unchanged whether
the visible layer is represented with binary or categorical (Potts) units,
with the categorical representation performing modestly better throughout.
\end{abstract}

\section{Motivation}

Many complex systems of interest in statistical physics are not single
homogeneous systems but collections of subsystems that interact weakly
with one another: coupled spin lattices, interacting populations of
neurons, or correlated sets of biological sequences organised into
families. A recurring question in this setting is inferential rather than
dynamical: given only samples of the joint configuration, can one
identify, or at least confirm the existence of, a genuine cross-subsystem
coupling, as opposed to a spurious correlation induced by having estimated
the single-subsystem statistics imperfectly?

We study this question on a toy model where the answer is known by
construction: two Ising subsystems, $A$ and $B$, each with an
independently generated sparse random coupling matrix, joined by a sparse
matrix of inter-family couplings. We generate equilibrium samples from
this joint model and ask a generative, energy-based learning model --- a
Restricted Boltzmann Machine (RBM) --- to recover the joint distribution.
Rather than training a single RBM from scratch on the joint data, we
deliberately split the problem: we first let two independent RBMs learn
each family on its own, and then ask a small set of \emph{additional}
hidden units, added on top of the two frozen models, to account for
whatever the two independent models fail to explain -- namely, the
inter-family correlations. This construction lets us isolate, quite
literally, the channel through which the model is allowed to represent
cross-family information, and to check directly, by comparison with the
known ground truth, whether that channel is doing its job.

\section{The two-family Ising model}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Hamiltonian}

Each family $X\in\{A,B\}$ consists of $N=30$ Ising spins
$s^X_i=\pm1$, $i=1,\dots,N$. The joint system of $2N=60$ spins is governed
by the Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}(\bm{s}_A,\bm{s}_B) = -\sum_{i<j} J^A_{ij}\,s^A_i s^A_j
-\sum_{i<j} J^B_{ij}\,s^B_i s^B_j
-\sum_{i,j} K_{ij}\,s^A_i s^B_j
-\sum_i h^A_i s^A_i -\sum_i h^B_i s^B_i \, ,
\label{eq:hamiltonian}
\end{equation}
sampled at inverse temperature $\beta = 0.5$. The intra-family couplings
$J^A$ and $J^B$ are built independently on sparse random graphs with edge
density $0.12$ (roughly $52$ non-zero bonds out of $\binom{30}{2}=435$
possible pairs in each family), with bond strengths drawn uniformly from
$J\in[-0.8,0.8]$ and small random local fields $h\in[-0.05,0.05]$. This is
an ordinary dilute Sherrington--Kirkpatrick-type random-bond Ising model
for each family separately, chosen only for concreteness -- the qualitative
picture we report does not depend on this specific choice of intra-family
disorder.

The inter-family coupling matrix $K\in\mathbb{R}^{30\times30}$ is the
object of interest. It is built as a \emph{sparse} matrix: a fixed number
$n_{\mathrm{links}}$ of $(i,j)$ pairs are chosen at random and assigned a
coupling strength drawn uniformly from $K_{ij}\in[-0.9,0.9]$; all other
entries of $K$ are exactly zero. We generated data sets at two link
densities, $n_{\mathrm{links}}=20$ ($2.2\%$ of the $900$ possible cross
pairs) and $n_{\mathrm{links}}=100$ ($11.1\%$). The sparser data set turns
out to sit uncomfortably close to a statistical noise floor discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:sparsity}, so throughout the rest of this report we use
the denser, $n_{\mathrm{links}}=100$, data set; the qualitative conclusions
carry over to the sparser case, only with a weaker signal-to-noise ratio.

\subsection{Equilibrium sampling}

Samples are drawn from the Boltzmann distribution
$p(\bm{s}_A,\bm{s}_B)\propto e^{-\beta\mathcal{H}}$ associated with
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hamiltonian} using single-spin-flip Metropolis--Hastings
dynamics, with $5000$ sweeps of burn-in and a further thinning of $100$
sweeps between recorded configurations, for a total of $20\,000$
independent joint configurations $(\bm{s}_A,\bm{s}_B)$. These are split
$70/15/15$ into training, validation and test sets. No information about
$J^A$, $J^B$ or $K$ is passed to the learning stage: the models below see
only the sampled spin configurations.

\section{Restricted Boltzmann Machines as the modelling tool}

An RBM is a pairwise energy-based model on a bipartite graph of visible
units $\bm{v}$ (here, the physical spins) and hidden (auxiliary) units
$\bm{h}$, with joint Boltzmann weight
\begin{equation}
p(\bm{v},\bm{h}) \propto \exp\!\big[-E(\bm{v},\bm{h})\big], \qquad
E(\bm{v},\bm{h}) = -\bm{v}^\top W \bm{h} - a(\bm{v}) - b(\bm{h}) \, ,
\end{equation}
where $W$ is a visible--hidden weight matrix and $a$, $b$ are
layer-specific potentials. The absence of visible-visible and
hidden-hidden couplings is precisely what makes both conditionals,
$p(\bm{h}\mid\bm{v})$ and $p(\bm{v}\mid\bm{h})$, factorised and hence
efficient to sample: the hidden layer plays the role of an auxiliary field
that mediates effective, non-pairwise interactions among the visible
spins once it is marginalised out, in the same spirit as a
Hubbard--Stratonovich decoupling of a many-body interaction. This is what
makes an RBM more expressive than an independent-spin (mean-field) model
while remaining tractable.

We use two variants, differing only in how the visible layer encodes the
Ising spin and in the hidden-unit statistics:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Binary RBM.} Visible and hidden units are both Bernoulli
variables ($v_i, h_\mu \in\{0,1\}$), the standard Hopfield-adjacent RBM.
\item \textbf{Potts RBM.} Visible units are one-hot (categorical, Potts)
variables -- the natural generalisation of an Ising spin to $q$ states,
here specialised to $q=2$ and sampled through a Gumbel-softmax
relaxation for efficient simulation -- paired with hidden units of the
\emph{dReLU} family (continuous, rectified, no-scale variant), which
generalise the Bernoulli hidden unit to a continuous field with a
richer response function.
\end{itemize}

Both variants are wrapped with an online re-standardisation of the visible
and hidden layers during training (zero mean, unit variance), a standard
trick that stabilises the gradient at no cost to the represented
distribution, and, for the Potts layer only, a zero-sum gauge fixing of
the categorical weights.

\subsection{Training}

Maximum-likelihood training of an RBM requires the gradient of the
log-likelihood, which contains a data-averaged and a model-averaged term;
the latter is estimated by persistent Monte Carlo chains (Persistent
Contrastive Divergence, PCD), i.e.\ a set of chains that are not
reinitialised at every gradient step but instead carried over and advanced
by a fixed number of Monte Carlo sweeps ($K=50$ sweeps here) between
successive parameter updates. This is simply stochastic gradient ascent
on the log-likelihood with the intractable partition-function term
estimated on the fly by simulation, using mini-batches of $512$
configurations and the Adam adaptive step-size rule. Each individual RBM
(one per family) is trained for $10\,000$ such gradient steps.

\section{The paired construction: frozen and added units}
\label{sec:paired}

The central question is whether cross-family correlations can be
attributed to a well-defined, minimal subset of model parameters, rather
than requiring a full joint re-training. We construct a single, larger
RBM -- the \emph{paired} model -- as follows (Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}):

\begin{enumerate}
\item The visible layer of the paired model is the concatenation of the
two families' visible layers, $60$ spins in total.
\item The hidden layer is the concatenation of three blocks: the $30$
hidden units of the independently trained $A$-model, the $30$ hidden
units of the independently trained $B$-model, and $30$ newly
\emph{added} hidden units with no prior identity.
\item The weight blocks connecting $A$'s hidden units to $A$'s visible
units, and $B$'s hidden units to $B$'s visible units, are initialised
from the independently trained models and then \emph{frozen}: after
every gradient step of the paired model's training, these blocks (and
the corresponding visible/hidden biases) are reset to their reference
values, discarding whatever gradient update the optimiser proposed for
them. This is a projection-based freezing scheme, equivalent in effect to
excluding these parameters from the gradient, but implemented as a
reset for convenience.
\item The weight blocks connecting $A$'s hidden units to $B$'s visible
units, and vice versa, are pinned at exactly zero throughout training:
the private hidden units of one family have, by construction, no direct
view of the other family's spins.
\item The only parameters left genuinely free to adapt under gradient
ascent are the weights connecting the $30$ added hidden units to the
\emph{full} $60$-spin visible layer, together with the added units' own
biases.
\end{enumerate}

By construction, any cross-family statistical dependence that the paired
model is capable of representing must flow through the added units: they
are the sole channel connecting $A$ and $B$. If the added units learn
nothing, the paired model is exactly equivalent, in its predictions, to
two independent, non-interacting RBMs. If they learn something, this
something can only be inter-family structure, since everything else in
the model is already fixed to reproduce single-family statistics as well
as the private models already do. The paired model is trained for a
further $3000$ gradient steps, on top of the fully-trained private
models, with the same PCD/Adam recipe.

