\section{Diagnostics}

We use two complementary levels of validation.

\paragraph{Training-time diagnostics.} During training of the paired
model we periodically compare, along the same short persistent Monte
Carlo chains used for the gradient estimate, data-averaged and
model-averaged correlations $\langle v_i h_\mu\rangle$ and
$\langle v_i v_j\rangle$, reporting the Pearson correlation $r$ between
the data-side and model-side vectors. These are cheap, on-line
diagnostics of convergence, not a substitute for a proper equilibrium
test, since the persistent chains used during training are advanced by
only a handful of sweeps per step and are not guaranteed to be
equilibrated.

\paragraph{Equilibrium validation.} The decisive test is performed
\emph{after} training, on the frozen, converged model: we draw $1000$
independent Gibbs chains, each run for $5000$ sweeps from a random
initial condition, and use the resulting equilibrium samples to estimate
the model's cross-family correlation matrix
$\langle s_i^A s_j^B\rangle_{\rm model}$. This is then compared with the
true, hidden coupling matrix $K$ via (i) the fraction of the $100$
strongest true couplings that are also among the $100$ strongest
correlations recovered by the model (``top-$K$ overlap''), and (ii) the
Pearson correlation between the full recovered correlation matrix and
$K$. Both quantities are also computed for the finite-sample data
themselves (an upper bound set by sampling noise) and for a null model
consisting of the two independently trained RBMs run side by side with no
shared units at all (a lower bound corresponding to zero learned
cross-family coupling).

\subsection{An important caveat on sparsity}
\label{sec:sparsity}

Because $K$ is very sparse (at most $100$ non-zero entries out of $900$),
the whole-matrix Pearson correlation between any noisy estimate of the
cross-correlation matrix and $K$ is structurally bounded well below $1$,
even for a perfect model: correlating a mostly-zero signal against a
dense, noisy estimate dilutes the true signal with sampling noise on all
the entries that are exactly zero by construction. We verified this
directly: restricting the same comparison to only the $100$ truly
non-zero entries of $K$ raises the correlation from $r=0.824$ to
$r=0.992$ using the training data alone. The relevant benchmark is
therefore not $r=1$ but the ceiling set by the finite-sample data
themselves, against which the paired model should be judged.

\section{Results}

We report results for a single representative configuration: the denser
inter-family coupling data set ($n_{\mathrm{links}}=100$), $30$ hidden
units per private RBM, $30$ added units, no L1/L2 regularisation on the
weights. The private RBMs are trained for $10\,000$ steps and the paired
model for a further $3000$ steps, for both the Potts and the Binary
variant of the architecture.

\subsection{Individual RBM fidelity and training dynamics}

Before any pairing, each private RBM is checked against held-out data.
Pseudo-likelihood on the training and validation sets track each other
closely for both families (Potts: $\ell=-0.598$ train vs.\ $-0.592$
validation for family $A$; Binary: $-0.598$ vs.\ $-0.598$), indicating no
overfitting at this training length (Fig.~\ref{fig:pll}). The
visible--hidden correlation check ($r=0.94$ Potts, $r=0.87$ Binary) is
systematically higher than the harder visible--visible check ($r=0.82$
Potts, $r=0.82$ Binary), as expected: reproducing the hidden units'
activation statistics is a softer target than reproducing the full
pairwise correlation structure of the spins themselves.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/pseudolikelihood.png}
\caption{Training and validation pseudo-likelihood of the private RBMs
(family $A$, family $B$) and of the paired RBM, as a function of gradient
step, Potts (top) and Binary (bottom) variants.}
\label{fig:pll}
\end{figure}

Once the paired model is assembled and its added units begin training,
the freeze check confirms that the private blocks remain exactly at their
reference values throughout ($30/30$ frozen parameters unchanged at every
logged step, for both variants). The added units' own visible--hidden
correlation grows steadily over the $3000$ paired-training steps
(Fig.~\ref{fig:abcheck}) and their weight norm increases by more than an
order of magnitude ($\times 14.9$ for Potts, $\times 9.7$ for Binary,
from initialisation), while the gradient on this block remains
non-negligible at the end of training -- i.e., the added units are still
actively adapting rather than having saturated, and no unit has collapsed
to a dead, always-on or always-off state.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/ab_check_training.png}
\caption{Evolution, over the $3000$ paired-training steps, of the
Pearson correlation between data-side and model-side interfamily
correlations $\langle v_i^A v_j^B\rangle$ (the ``$ab$-check''), for the
Potts and Binary paired models. Both climb from a poorly correlated
initial state ($K_{AB}=0$, independent-model regime) towards
$r\approx0.8$.}
\label{fig:abcheck}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Equilibrium validation against the ground truth}

The training-time diagnostics above are encouraging but not conclusive,
since they are measured on short, non-equilibrated persistent chains. We
now turn to the decisive test: long, properly burnt-in Gibbs sampling from
the final, frozen paired model (Section~\ref{sec:paired}), and comparison
with the true coupling matrix $K$.

As a first sanity check, Fig.~\ref{fig:freeenergy} shows the free energy
of the sampled chains as a function of the number of Monte Carlo sweeps,
confirming that $5000$ sweeps is comfortably sufficient for the chains to
reach a stationary regime before the burn-in samples are discarded.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/free_energy.png}
\caption{Free energy of the Gibbs chains as a function of the number of
Monte Carlo sweeps, averaged over $1000$ independent chains, for the
private and paired Potts models. A stationary plateau is reached well
before the $5000$-sweep burn-in used throughout.}
\label{fig:freeenergy}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:blockhv} decomposes the same equilibrium samples by hidden
block, exactly mirroring the architecture of Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}:
the frozen $A$--$A$ and $B$--$B$ blocks reproduce the data almost exactly
($\rho=0.95$ and $\rho=0.97$), confirming that pairing does not disturb
what the private models had already learned, while the added units'
correlation with the \emph{full} joint visible layer, $\langle
h_{\mathrm{add}}\, v_{\mathrm{full}}\rangle$, tracks the data-side
estimate at $\rho=0.997$: the added units are not merely active, they are
active in a way that is faithful to the data.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/block_hv.png}
\caption{Data- vs.\ model-side visible--hidden correlations
$\langle v h\rangle$, from equilibrium Gibbs samples of the Potts paired
model, split by hidden block: private $A$--$A$ and $B$--$B$ blocks
(``should reproduce the data essentially exactly'', since they are
frozen), and the added units against the full joint visible layer
(the only block that can encode genuinely new, cross-family information).}
\label{fig:blockhv}
\end{figure}

The central result of this study is the direct comparison, at equilibrium,
between the model's recovered cross-family correlation matrix and the
true coupling matrix $K$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:crosscorr}), summarised
quantitatively in Table~\ref{tab:headline}. The paired model recovers
$67\%$ (Potts) and $65\%$ (Binary) of the $100$ strongest true couplings
among its own $100$ strongest recovered correlations, compared to a
$74\%$ recovery rate for the training data themselves (the practical
ceiling given the finite sample size) and essentially chance-level
recovery ($6$--$8\%$) for two independent, uncoupled RBMs. The
whole-matrix correlation against $K$ reaches $r=0.796$ (Potts) and
$r=0.786$ (Binary), against a data ceiling of $r=0.844$ and an
independent-model baseline consistent with zero ($r=0.012$ Potts,
$r=-0.039$ Binary). In other words, the $30$ added units, despite having
no privileged information about which cross-family pairs are actually
coupled, recover the large majority of the true coupling structure,
closing most of the gap between ``no shared units at all'' and ``as good
as the data allow''.

\begin{table}[htb]
\centering
\caption{Equilibrium validation against the true coupling matrix $K$
(Gibbs samples, $1000$ chains, $5000$ sweeps burn-in). ``Data'' is the
finite-sample ceiling; ``independent'' is the null model of two
uncoupled, independently trained RBMs.}
\label{tab:headline}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & paired model & data (ceiling) & independent (null) \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Potts + dReLU}} \\
\quad top-100 overlap & 0.67 & 0.74 & 0.06 \\
\quad corr.\ with $K$ & 0.796 & 0.844 & 0.012 \\
\addlinespace
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\textit{Binary}} \\
\quad top-100 overlap & 0.65 & 0.74 & 0.08 \\
\quad corr.\ with $K$ & 0.786 & 0.844 & $-0.039$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

