\label{fig:crosscorr}
\end{figure}

Finally, we checked that this recovery of cross-family structure does not
come at the cost of corrupting each family's own private statistics
(Fig.~\ref{fig:familypreservation}): the residual off-diagonal
correlation within each family, when sampled from the full paired model,
stays close to its data value (family $A$: $0.057\to0.068$ for Potts,
$0.057\to0.068$ for Binary; family $B$: $0.048\to0.058$ Potts,
$0.048\to0.060$ Binary), a small degradation consistent with the added
units introducing weak, indirect, effective intra-family couplings
through the shared hidden layer, rather than with any breakdown of the
frozen private blocks.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{figures/family_preservation.png}
\caption{Intra-family correlation matrices, data vs.\ paired-model
equilibrium samples, family $A$ and family $B$ separately. The frozen
blocks preserve the private structure learned before pairing, up to a
small perturbation induced by the shared added units.}
\label{fig:familypreservation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Potts versus Binary}

Across every diagnostic, the Potts + dReLU variant performs modestly but
consistently better than the plain Binary RBM: the private RBMs reach a
higher visible--hidden correlation ($r=0.94$ vs.\ $0.87$), the added
units track the data far more closely at equilibrium ($\rho=0.997$ vs.\
$\rho=0.81$ for $\langle h_{\mathrm{add}} v_{\mathrm{full}}\rangle$), yet
this advantage barely shows up in the final, decisive test against
ground truth (top-$K$ overlap $0.67$ vs.\ $0.65$, correlation with $K$
$0.796$ vs.\ $0.786$). This suggests that the categorical (Potts)
representation, being a more natural encoding of a two-state spin than a
$\{0,1\}$ Bernoulli unit combined with a continuous dReLU hidden
response, yields cleaner, more faithful internal statistics -- but that
both representations are, in this regime, expressive enough to extract
essentially the same amount of genuine cross-family signal once
equilibrated.

\section{Discussion and outlook}

The main qualitative finding is that a small, dedicated block of hidden
units, added on top of two frozen, independently trained models, is
sufficient to recover the large majority of an otherwise hidden
inter-family coupling structure, and that this recovery can be confirmed
directly against the known ground truth through equilibrium Monte Carlo
sampling rather than inferred indirectly from training curves alone. Two
points of caution are worth stating explicitly. First, the density of the
inter-family coupling matters: at $n_{\mathrm{links}}=20$ (roughly a
fifth of the density used above) the same construction still recovers
correlation with $K$ well above the independent-model baseline, but the
absolute values of the top-$K$ overlap and of $r$ against $K$ are
noticeably weaker, indicating that very sparse, weak cross-couplings are
harder to disentangle from sampling noise with a fixed amount of
training. Second, the natural whole-matrix Pearson correlation against a
sparse ground truth carries a structural ceiling well below $1$
(Sec.~\ref{sec:sparsity}); reporting the recovered value against the
finite-sample data's own ceiling, as we did throughout, is essential to
avoid over- or under-stating how well the model has actually done.

We regard this as a controlled, minimal test bed rather than a finished
inference method: a single realisation of the random couplings and a
single training run were used throughout, without averaging over seeds or
a systematic scan of $\beta$, hidden-layer size, or training length.
Section~\ref{sec:hadd-sweep} takes up the most immediate of these open
questions -- a systematic scan over the added-unit count itself -- and
finds that getting cross-family recovery right, and avoiding a subtler
side effect on each family's own single-site statistics, trade off
against each other in a way invisible from any single configuration.

\section{How many added units are needed? A capacity sweep}
\label{sec:hadd-sweep}

Every result so far used $H_{\mathrm{add}}=30$ added units, a fixed choice
carried through without justification beyond ``comparable to each
family's own $30$ hidden units''. Since the added units are the sole
channel through which the paired model can represent cross-family
information (Section~\ref{sec:paired}), it is natural to ask how many are
actually needed, and whether there is a cost -- not to the headline
recovery, but elsewhere -- to using more or fewer than that.

\subsection{A stronger local field, to make single-site diagnostics informative}
\label{sec:strongfield}

Answering this requires trusting a diagnostic finer than the pairwise
correlation checks used above: the single-site marginal $\langle v_i
\rangle$, the simplest possible observable of the visible layer. Under
the original data set (Section~\ref{sec:model}, field range
$h\in[-0.05,0.05]$), this observable turns out to be structurally
unusable for that purpose. The genuine site-to-site variation in
$\langle v_i\rangle$ has standard deviation $\approx0.008$ across the $30$
sites of a family -- a direct consequence of the narrow field range -- while
the sampling error of a mean estimated from $N$ Gibbs samples is
$\sqrt{0.25/N}$: $\approx0.022$ at the $512$-sample training batches used
throughout, and still $\approx0.016$ at the $1000$-sample equilibrium
validation of Section~\ref{sec:paired}. The genuine signal is smaller
than the noise floor of either diagnostic by construction, regardless of
how well the model has been trained: no amount of additional Monte Carlo
sampling changes this without also enlarging the training set itself,
since the data-side estimate carries its own, comparably-sized, finite-sample
error.

We therefore generated a second data set, identical in every other
respect (edge density $0.12$, $J\in[-0.8,0.8]$, $K\in[-0.9,0.9]$,
$n_{\mathrm{links}}=100$, $\beta=0.5$) but with the field range widened
to $h\in[-0.3,0.3]$, raising the genuine $\langle v_i\rangle$ signal to
standard deviation $\approx0.045$ -- comfortably above both noise floors --
without touching the intra- or inter-family coupling structure that is
the actual object of interest. All results in this section use this
wider-field data set.

\subsection{Two distinct failure modes for the single-site marginal}
\label{sec:v-failure-modes}

Widening the field resolves the private RBMs' $\langle v_i\rangle$
agreement almost completely ($\rho\approx0.99$ at equilibrium, essentially
indistinguishable from the excellent $\langle v_ih_\mu\rangle$ and
$\langle v_iv_j\rangle$ agreement already reported in
Section~\ref{sec:model}), confirming that this observable was never
measuring a model deficiency -- only a noise floor. This is the same
qualitative resolution as reweighting sample size: on the original,
narrow-field data set, raising the equilibrium sample count from $1000$ to
$10\,000$ likewise took the private RBMs' $\langle v_i\rangle$ agreement
from $\rho=0.12/0.39$ to $\rho=0.80/0.68$ (family $A$/$B$). Both routes --
more samples, or a stronger genuine signal -- fix the same problem, because
it is the same problem: insufficient signal-to-noise in a finite-sample
estimate.

The \emph{paired} model's own $\langle v_i\rangle$, however, does not
respond to either fix. At $H_{\mathrm{add}}=30$ on the wider-field data
set, $\rho(\langle v\rangle_{\mathrm{data}},\langle
v\rangle_{\mathrm{model}})=0.26$ at $N=1000$ equilibrium samples and
$\rho=0.24$ at $N=50\,000$ -- a fifty-fold increase in samples changes
nothing. Examining the residual $\langle v\rangle_{\mathrm{model}}-\langle
v\rangle_{\mathrm{data}}$ directly at $N=50\,000$ confirms this is not a
sampling artefact: its mean is zero (no overall shift), but its standard
deviation, $\approx0.10$, exceeds the genuine signal's own standard
deviation ($\approx0.045$) by more than a factor of two. This is a real,
stable discrepancy between the paired model's marginals and the data's,
not a measurement problem.

The mechanism is structural rather than a training failure. The frozen
$A$--$A$ and $B$--$B$ blocks are exact copies of the independently trained
private RBMs' parameters (freeze check: $30/30$ logged steps OK, at
every $H_{\mathrm{add}}$ reported below), so the only parameters that can
move a site's marginal away from what its private model alone would
predict are the added units. But a hidden unit cannot encode a
correlation between two visible sites without also nudging each site's
own marginal: the same weight that lets the unit fire preferentially when
sites $i$ and $j$ align is, by construction, a weight that shifts $i$'s
and $j$'s individual expectations whenever the unit is active at all. The
two effects are not separable. We had initially guessed this collateral
shift would be roughly independent of field strength (its standard
deviation measured $\approx0.015$ on the original, narrow-field data set)
-- but it is not: on the wider-field data it grows to $\approx0.10$, a
factor of $\approx7$, \emph{faster} than the $\approx5.6\times$ growth of
the genuine signal itself. A plausible reading is that pushing a more
strongly polarised site toward a correlated outcome requires
proportionally larger weight than pushing a nearly-unbiased one, so the
same correlational job costs more collateral marginal disturbance once
the background field is stronger.

\subsection{Sweeping the added-unit count}

We repeated the full private-plus-paired pipeline (unchanged protocol:
$10\,000$ PCD steps per private RBM, $3000$ further steps for the paired
model, $1000$-sample equilibrium Gibbs validation) at
$H_{\mathrm{add}}\in\{5,10,20,30,40,50,60\}$ on the wider-field data set,
holding every other hyperparameter fixed.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures/hadd_sweep_recovery.png}
\caption{Cross-family recovery against the true coupling matrix $K$, as a
function of the number of added units $H_{\mathrm{add}}$ (wider-field data
set, equilibrium Gibbs samples, $N=1000$). Both the top-$100$
strongest-pair overlap and the whole-matrix correlation with $K$ rise
steeply up to $H_{\mathrm{add}}\approx30$ and then plateau, within noise,
all the way to $H_{\mathrm{add}}=60$.}
\label{fig:haddrecovery}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:haddrecovery} shows the headline recovery metrics: both
climb steeply from $H_{\mathrm{add}}=5$ (top-$100$ overlap $0.32$,
correlation with $K$ $0.48$) to $H_{\mathrm{add}}=30$ (overlap $0.69$,
correlation $0.81$), then plateau completely -- $40$, $50$ and $60$ added
units all sit within noise of the $30$-unit result on both metrics.
Thirty added units already recover essentially everything extractable
from a $100$-link coupling matrix of this size and strength; beyond that
point, more units buy nothing on the metric that actually motivated their
existence.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures/hadd_sweep_collateral.png}
\caption{Collateral effect on single-site statistics, same sweep as
Fig.~\ref{fig:haddrecovery}: the paired model's own $\langle v\rangle$
agreement (left) and the frozen private blocks' $\langle
h_Av_A\rangle$/$\langle h_Bv_B\rangle$ agreement, averaged (right), both
as a function of $H_{\mathrm{add}}$. The orange diamond and dotted
connector at $H_{\mathrm{add}}=20$ show a full rerun with an independent
random seed, illustrating the size of the run-to-run noise on these two
diagnostics.}
\label{fig:haddcollateral}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:haddcollateral} tells a different, non-monotonic story for
the collateral cost on single-site statistics. Both the paired model's own
$\langle v\rangle$ agreement and the frozen blocks' $\langle
hv\rangle$ agreement are good at both extremes of the sweep --
$H_{\mathrm{add}}=5,10$, where the added units are capacity-starved and
simply are not pushing hard (confirmed independently by their poor
recovery score at the same points), and $H_{\mathrm{add}}=60$, well past
the point where more capacity helps recovery -- and worst in the middle,
close to where the headline recovery curve is completing its climb to
the plateau ($H_{\mathrm{add}}=20$--$50$).

Because each of these $\rho$ estimates is computed over only the $30$ (or,
for $\langle v\rangle$, $60$) sites of one or two families, they carry
substantial run-to-run variance. We checked this directly by rerunning
the entire $H_{\mathrm{add}}=20$ configuration with an independent random
seed: the paired model's $\langle v\rangle$ agreement moved from
$\rho=0.07$ to $\rho=0.68$, and the frozen blocks' average $\langle
hv\rangle$ agreement from $\rho=0.70$ to $\rho=0.86$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:haddcollateral},
orange diamonds) -- confirming that any single point on this curve should
be read cautiously. The broader shape, however, is not simply an artefact
of that one noisy point: $H_{\mathrm{add}}=30$, $40$ and $50$ form an
independent cluster of three low values, distinct from the consistently
higher values at $5$, $10$ and $60$, so the qualitative U-shape -- good at
the edges, worse near full capacity utilisation -- is better supported
than any individual number on the curve.

\subsection{Interpretation and a design lesson}

The pattern is consistent with the mechanism identified in
Section~\ref{sec:v-failure-modes}: the collateral marginal shift tracks
how hard the added units are actually being pushed, which is governed by
the correlational workload relative to their number, not by their count
alone. Below the capacity needed for the task, the added units are not
yet working hard (and, the recovery curve confirms, genuinely cannot
represent more correlation with so few of them); once comfortably past
that point, the same, now-saturated correlational workload is shared
across more hidden units, so each one is pushed less and disturbs the
single-site marginals less.

This suggests a concrete design lesson for the setting that motivated this
model in the first place (Section~\ref{sec:protein}): the point of
``just enough'' added-unit capacity for the recovery task is exactly the
worst place to sit if a clean model, undistorted at the level of each
subsystem's own statistics, also matters. Once a rough estimate of the
required capacity is available, over-provisioning added units costs
essentially nothing on the metric that motivates them, while measurably
improving every other diagnostic. Because the right $H_{\mathrm{add}}$ for
real protein families is not known in advance -- unlike here, where the
sweep could be run against a known ground truth -- generous
over-provisioning is a cheap, low-risk default for that setting.

\section{Generalization to real protein sequence data}
\label{sec:protein}

The two-family Ising model of Sections~\ref{sec:model}--\ref{sec:paired} is
a controlled test case with a known ground truth, but the motivating
question -- can inter-subsystem correlations be attributed to a small,
dedicated block of hidden units, added on top of independently trained
private models? -- is really about correlated biological sequence
families. We therefore repeat the entire construction, unchanged in its
architecture and training recipe, on a real protein data set: pairs of
sequences drawn from two Pfam domains, PF00072 and PF00512, that co-occur
in the same protein (a standard two-component signal-transduction
receiver/histidine-kinase pairing), giving $77\,880$ paired sequences from
\texttt{PF00072\_PF00512\_paired.fasta}. Family $A$ is the first
$111$ alignment columns (PF00072) and family $B$ the remaining $66$
columns (PF00512) of each paired sequence; visible units are one-hot,
$q=21$-state Potts variables (the $20$ amino acids plus the alignment gap
symbol), encoded exactly as in Section~\ref{sec:model}'s Potts variant but
with $q=21$ in place of $q=2$. Sequences are split $70/15/15$ into
training, validation and (unused) test sets, mirroring the Ising study.

The private RBMs use $150$ (family $A$) and $100$ (family $B$) hidden
units -- family $A$'s domain has nearly twice as many columns, so it is
given proportionally more capacity -- trained for $10\,000$ PCD steps each
with $K=50$ Gibbs sweeps per step, exactly as in Section~\ref{sec:model};
the paired model adds $H_{\mathrm{add}}=20$ free hidden units on top of
the two frozen private blocks and is trained for a further $5000$ steps,
using the identical freeze-and-reset mechanism of Section~\ref{sec:paired}.

\subsection{Two adaptations required by real data}

Two aspects of the Ising validation pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:model},
`Diagnostics') do not carry over to real sequences and had to be replaced.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{No ground-truth coupling matrix.} $K$ was known by
construction in the Ising model; no such object exists for real protein
families. We instead use the held-out validation sequences (never seen
during training) as the best available truth proxy for ``what real
cross-family covariation looks like'', and report the training data's own
agreement with that held-out split as the finite-sample-noise ceiling --
the direct analogue of the Section~\ref{sec:sparsity} ``data ceiling''.
\item \textbf{Amino acids are not ordinal.} The Ising validation reduces
each spin to a single $\pm1$ number before taking a Pearson correlation.
Amino-acid identities have no natural order, so an analogous numbering
($A=0,C=1,\dots$) and correlation would depend on that arbitrary choice.
We instead measure site-pair coupling strength by the Frobenius norm, over
the $q\times q$ colour pairs, of the connected one-hot correlation tensor
$C[a,i,b,j]=\langle v_i^{=a} v_j^{=b}\rangle-\langle v_i^{=a}\rangle\langle
v_j^{=b}\rangle$ -- the same quantity already used, in scalar-summary
form, by the training-time $vh$/$vv$/$ab$ diagnostics, and the standard way
of reducing a Potts model's per-site-pair coupling to a single
non-negative covariation score (cf.\ Frobenius-norm/APC scores in direct
coupling analysis).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Results}

Training-time diagnostics reproduce the qualitative Ising picture: both
private RBMs show no train/validation pseudo-likelihood gap worth noting
($\ell=-1.08$ train vs.\ $-1.18$ validation for family $A$; $-0.91$ vs.\
$-0.96$ for family $B$), the freeze check confirms the private blocks stay
exactly at their reference values throughout paired training ($50/50$
logged steps OK), and the interfamily $ab$-check on the training-time PCD
chains climbs steadily over the $5000$ paired steps
(Fig.~\ref{fig:pfamabcheck}) -- but only to $r\approx0.61$, distinctly
below the $r\approx0.8$ reached on the synthetic Ising data in the same
number of steps.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.42\linewidth]{figures/pfam_ab_check_training.png}
\caption{Protein data set: evolution of the training-time interfamily
$ab$-check (cf.\ Fig.~\ref{fig:abcheck}) over the $5000$ paired-training
steps. It climbs but plateaus around $r\approx0.6$, short of the $r\approx0.8$
reached on the synthetic Ising data.}
\label{fig:pfamabcheck}
\end{figure}

The decisive, post-training equilibrium test draws $1000$ Gibbs chains,
each run for $25\,000$ sweeps (five times longer than the Ising study, to
accommodate the much larger $q=21$, $177$-site state space), and compares
against the held-out reference as described above. The paired model
clearly separates from the zero-coupling independent-RBM baseline -- a
top-$366$ ($5\%$ of the $111\times66$ cross-family site pairs)
strongest-pair overlap of $0.139$ against $0.027$ for the independent
baseline, and a whole-matrix correlation against the held-out reference of
$r=0.404$ against $r=0.177$ (Table~\ref{tab:pfamheadline},
Fig.~\ref{fig:pfamcrosscorr}) -- confirming that the added units do
recover genuine, verifiable cross-family coevolutionary signal on real
sequence data, not only on the idealized synthetic model. However, both
figures sit markedly further from the training-data ceiling ($0.839$
overlap, $r=0.966$) than in the Ising case, where the paired model closed
most of the gap to that same ceiling (Table~\ref{tab:headline}).

\begin{table}[htb]
\centering
\caption{Equilibrium validation against the held-out reference (Gibbs
samples, $1000$ chains, $25\,000$ sweeps burn-in). ``Train data'' is the
finite-sample ceiling (agreement between the training set and the
held-out split); ``independent'' is the null model of two uncoupled,
independently trained RBMs. Cf.\ Table~\ref{tab:headline} for the Ising
analogue.}
\label{tab:pfamheadline}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & paired model & train data (ceiling) & independent (null) \\
\midrule
top-366 overlap & 0.139 & 0.839 & 0.027 \\
corr.\ with held-out & 0.404 & 0.966 & 0.177 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/pfam_cross_corr.png}
\caption{Cross-family coupling-strength matrices (Frobenius-norm score,
see text) at equilibrium: held-out data, training data, paired model, and
two independent, uncoupled RBMs. The paired model's pattern is visibly
distinct from the independent baseline's but only partially matches the
held-out reference's finer structure.}
\label{fig:pfamcrosscorr}
\end{figure}

Block-wise, the added units' correlation with the full joint visible layer
is high and faithful to the data ($\rho=0.914$ for
$\langle h_{\mathrm{add}}v_{\mathrm{full}}\rangle$; $\rho=0.896$ and
$\rho=0.942$ restricted to family $A$ and $B$ separately), essentially
matching the quality seen in the Ising study. In sharp contrast, the
\emph{frozen} private blocks -- which reproduced the data almost exactly
in the Ising case ($\rho=0.95$--$0.97$, Fig.~\ref{fig:blockhv}) -- show
visibly degraded agreement here ($\rho=0.50$ for $\langle h_A v_A\rangle$,
$\rho=0.75$ for $\langle h_B v_B\rangle$; Fig.~\ref{fig:pfamblockhv}), and
the family-preservation check tells the same story: the mean off-diagonal
coupling strength within each family grows substantially once sampled
from the full paired model relative to its data value (family $A$:
$0.038\to0.135$; family $B$: $0.064\to0.123$ -- a $2$--$3.5\times$
increase, against a barely-perceptible change in the Ising case).

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/pfam_block_hv.png}
\caption{Protein data set: data- vs.\ model-side visible--hidden
correlations from equilibrium Gibbs samples, split by hidden block (cf.\
Fig.~\ref{fig:blockhv}). The added units (green) track the data
closely; the frozen private blocks (blue, orange) -- which matched almost
exactly in the Ising study -- show visible, systematic disagreement.}
\label{fig:pfamblockhv}
\end{figure}

\subsection{An important caveat on equilibration}
\label{sec:pfam-equilibration}

Before over-interpreting this degradation as a property of the trained
model, we checked the same free-energy burn-in diagnostic used in
Fig.~\ref{fig:freeenergy}. Unlike the Ising chains, which reach a visible
plateau well inside their $5000$-sweep budget, the protein chains are
\emph{still decreasing} at the end of their $25\,000$-sweep run
(Fig.~\ref{fig:pfamfreeenergy}), for all three models (private $A$,
private $B$, and paired). The much larger, $q=21$ categorical state space
of a $\sim\!100$-site protein domain evidently mixes far more slowly under
single-site Gibbs sampling than the $30$-spin Ising family, and $25\,000$
sweeps -- already five times the Ising burn-in -- is not enough to reach a
genuine stationary distribution here. The equilibrium comparisons above
should therefore be read as a lower bound obtained from an
still-relaxing, rather than a fully converged, sample: both the apparent
family-preservation degradation and the shortfall relative to the
training-data ceiling could tighten with substantially longer chains, and
neither should yet be taken as a settled property of the trained model
itself.

\begin{figure}[htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/pfam_free_energy.png}
\caption{Free energy of the Gibbs chains as a function of the number of
Monte Carlo sweeps, protein data set (cf.\ Fig.~\ref{fig:freeenergy}). Unlike
the Ising case, no plateau is reached within the $25\,000$-sweep budget used
throughout the equilibrium validation above.}
\label{fig:pfamfreeenergy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Discussion}

The freeze-and-add construction generalizes qualitatively from the
synthetic Ising toy model to real, biologically evolved protein sequence
families: a small ($H_{\mathrm{add}}=20$) shared hidden block, added on
top of two independently trained, frozen private RBMs, recovers
cross-family coevolutionary structure that a pair of uncoupled models
cannot -- by a wide margin on every metric we checked. This confirms that
the mechanism is not an artifact specific to the clean, sparse synthetic
coupling matrix of Sections~\ref{sec:model}--\ref{sec:paired}.

Quantitatively, however, the protein case is a substantially harder
regime than the Ising toy model: the recovered signal sits much further
from its own finite-sample ceiling ($0.139/0.839$ top-$K$ overlap vs.\ the
Ising study's $0.67/0.74$), and the frozen private blocks show a
degradation under long equilibrium sampling that is essentially absent in
the Ising case. Section~\ref{sec:pfam-equilibration} shows that at least
part of this gap is very plausibly a sampling artifact rather than a
genuine limitation of the trained parameters: the protein chains have not
visibly equilibrated even after five times the Ising burn-in budget, so
comparisons drawn from them carry an unknown, one-sided bias. The
remaining, likely genuine contributors are (i) real protein coevolution is
denser and more structured than the sparse, uniformly-random synthetic
$K$ used in Sections~\ref{sec:model}--\ref{sec:paired}, which is a
harder target for a fixed, small $H_{\mathrm{add}}$; and (ii) $5000$
paired-training steps, sufficient for the $60$-spin Ising visible layer,
may simply undertrain a $20$-unit added block sitting on top of a $177$-site,
$q=21$ visible layer -- nearly six times as many free visible-unit degrees
of freedom for the same number of added hidden units and training steps.
Distinguishing a genuine ceiling on what this construction can recover on
real sequence data from these two confounds -- insufficient Gibbs mixing
and insufficient paired-training length -- is the natural next step before
drawing firm conclusions about its applicability beyond the controlled
Ising setting.

\end{document}
